@*
    Citations: a document id and a quoted excerpt from that document.

    THE SNIPPET IS UNTRUSTED VENDOR PROSE. It is written by the party being assessed, and one
    of the fixtures deliberately contains an injected instruction aimed at an automated
    reviewer. The engine's central property is that this text can never reach a risk score —
    but it does reach here, on purpose, because a reviewer must be able to read what a decision
    was based on.

    So this component is where that trust boundary now lands:

      - @citation.Snippet interpolates, which Blazor HTML-escapes. Never MarkupString, never
        an innerHTML interop. Escaping is the default; the rule is about not opting out of it.
      - The text is NOT filtered or redacted. Hiding the injected sentence would hide the very
        thing the system is demonstrating it handles safely.
      - It is visually marked as quoted, untrusted material so a reviewer does not read it as
        something the system asserts.

    Pinned by CitationListTests.
*@

<div class="oc-citations">
    <h3 class="oc-citations__heading">Citations</h3>

    @if (Citations is null || Citations.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="oc-empty">No citations.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <ul class="oc-citations__list">
            @foreach (var citation in Citations)
            {
                <li class="oc-citation">
                    <div class="oc-citation__id">@citation.DocumentId</div>
                    <blockquote class="oc-citation__snippet" data-untrusted="true">
                        @citation.Snippet
                    </blockquote>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
        <p class="oc-citations__note">
            Quoted from vendor-supplied evidence. Shown as written, and never interpreted.
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The citations backing the stated reasons.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<CitationDto>? Citations { get; set; }
}
